---
// Continental Tour · Voice of the Month —— Vol. 02 (English)
//
// Shares layouts/Magazine.astro with the Chinese page: one layout, so the two
// versions can never drift apart.
//
// Both photos are real Wikimedia Commons shots, self-hosted in public/magazine/:
// - hero: Mulatu Astatke in Berkeley (2024), TurquoiseGoose / CC BY-SA 4.0
//   original 4080×3072, cropped to the layout's 2.13:1 and squeezed to 1400×657 / 132KB
// - inline: a krar player in Wolayta, Rod Waddington / CC BY-SA 2.0
//   original 5530×4016, cropped to 2.2:1, 1280×581 / 80KB
// upload.wikimedia.org times out on some networks, and the hero is a first-paint element.
import Magazine from '../../layouts/Magazine.astro';

const hero = '/magazine/ethiopia-mulatu.jpg';
const inline = '/magazine/ethiopia-krar.jpg';

// 固定尺寸的分享图，理由见中文版 issue-02.astro 的同一处注释。
const ogImage = '/magazine/og-issue-02.jpg';

const ethiopiaHref = '/countries/ethiopia/';
const wikiUrl = 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mulatu_Astatke';
---

<Magazine
  title="The Pentatonic Nights of Addis Ababa: Mulatu Astatke and an Interrupted Jazz Age"
  description="Beyond the images of the 1984 famine, Ethiopia once had a jazz age of its own. Listen to how Mulatu Astatke folded Latin rhythm, bebop and a highland memory into five notes — and called it Ethio-jazz. Continental Tour · Voice of the Month, Vol. 02."
  lang="en"
  altHref="/magazine/issue-02/"
  altLabel="阅读中文版"
  kicker="Continental Tour · Voice of the Month"
  issue="Vol. 02 | Voices of the Horn"
  headline="The Pentatonic Nights of Addis Ababa: Mulatu Astatke and an Interrupted Jazz Age"
  readTime="8 min read"
  issueNav={{ href: '/magazine/issue-01-en/', label: 'Previous · Vol.01' }}
  ogImage={ogImage}
>
  <figure class="mag__hero" style="--hero-pos: 50% 45%">
    <img src={hero} alt="Mulatu Astatke singing into a microphone on stage, his band and congas lit in deep blue behind him" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" />
    <figcaption>
      Mulatu Astatke · Photo <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mulatu_Astatke_Berkeley_2024.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">TurquoiseGoose</a> / <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CC BY-SA 4.0</a>
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <blockquote>
    <p>When spider webs unite, they can tie up a lion.</p>
    <cite>Ethiopian proverb</cite>
  </blockquote>

  <div class="mag__body">
    <p class="mag__lead">
      For most of the world, Ethiopia froze in 1984. That year the cameras reached the camps in the
      north, and television audiences saw cracked earth and hungry children, followed by tens of
      thousands singing together at Live Aid. Ever since, the country has carried a single word in
      the international memory: famine.
    </p>
    <p>
      Turn the dial back twenty years, though — to Addis Ababa in 1966 — and you hear something else
      entirely. A highland city at 2,400 metres, cold at night, the air thin enough to make brass
      sound unusually bright. On the stages of its bars and clubs there were bands in suits, drummers
      playing Latin patterns, saxophones playing bebop. And behind the vibraphone, a young man was
      folding all of it into a different mode.
    </p>

    <h2>The man who retuned jazz to the shape of Ethiopia — Mulatu Astatke</h2>
    <p>
      Mulatu Astatke was born in 1943 in Jimma, in the south-western valleys where coffee comes from.
      His father wanted him to learn a useful trade and sent him to Wales to study engineering. There
      he heard jazz for the first time, and put the engineering down.
    </p>
    <p>
      He moved to Berklee in Boston, then a young school, and became one of its first African students.
    </p>
    <p>
      At Berklee he ran into a problem nobody else regarded as a problem. Ethiopian traditional music
      is pentatonic — five notes to the octave. Jazz harmony is built on twelve, with sevenths, ninths
      and altered tones packed in densely. Set Western chords under an Ethiopian melody and it always
      looks like a suit tailored over national dress.
    </p>
    <p>
      His solution was to invert the question: don't fit chords to the melody, treat the Ethiopian
      modes themselves as the harmony. Highland music has four qenet — tezeta (nostalgia), bati,
      ambassel, anchi hoye. Change the mode under the same tune and longing turns into warning. Mulatu
      laid those modes down as a bed of metallic vibraphone colour and let the horns and congas walk
      over them. That is what came to be called Ethio-jazz.
    </p>
    <p>
      He went home to Addis Ababa in 1966. The eight years that followed were the city's best, and
      people later gave them a name: Swinging Addis. In 1973 Duke Ellington's tour reached Addis, and
      Ellington stepped up to play with Mulatu's band — a man who had written jazz into the textbooks,
      standing on a stage in the African highlands.
    </p>
  </div>

  <figure class="mag__figure" style="--fig-pos: 50% 35%">
    <img src={inline} alt="An Ethiopian musician playing a krar in backlight by a window" loading="lazy" decoding="async" />
    <figcaption>
      A krar player in Wolayta · Photo <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Krar_Player,_Wolayta,_Ethiopia_(18547771378).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Rod Waddington</a> / <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CC BY-SA 2.0</a>
    </figcaption>
  </figure>

  <div class="mag__body">
    <h2>The instrument: the krar, and homesickness on five strings</h2>
    <p>
      To understand that five-note world, look at an instrument: the krar. A lyre with a bowl-shaped
      wooden body covered in hide, five or six strings, played with a plectrum.
    </p>
    <p>
      It is almost crude in its simplicity, and precisely because of that it shaped how an entire
      plateau hears. No semitones, no modulations to speak of: melody can only move through timbre,
      ornament and rhythm. A krar player will worry a single note — rubbing, bending, sliding — and
      put the whole emotion into the gaps between those five notes.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then there is the masenqo, a single-string bowed fiddle, thin and piercing, like someone crying
      far away. Singers deliberately let the voice sit against the string until you cannot tell which
      is the person and which is the instrument.
    </p>
    <p>
      So when Mulatu put a vibraphone on stage, he was doing something quietly radical: using a Western,
      metal, fixed-pitch instrument to take on the krar's role. Not replacing it — translating its
      atmosphere into another material.
    </p>

    <h2>1974: the door closes</h2>
    <p>
      1974. Emperor Haile Selassie is overthrown and the Derg takes power. Curfews, censorship,
      purges. The clubs close, the bands scatter, jazz is reclassified as Western decadence.
    </p>
    <p>
      Musicians left in numbers. Mulatu went to the United States and made a living teaching and
      arranging for twenty years. In those two decades his records sat in second-hand shops around
      the world, un-reissued, unmentioned. Swinging Addis became a decade without an archive.
    </p>
    <p>
      The turn came from a Frenchman. In the 1990s Francis Falceto began compiling a series for Buda
      Musique called Éthiopiques, digging out Ethiopian recordings of the 1960s and 70s one track at
      a time. Volume four was given over entirely to Mulatu.
    </p>
    <p>
      Then in 2005 Jim Jarmusch used his music in <em>Broken Flowers</em>. That suspended, semitone-free
      melody, set against Bill Murray's face on a sofa, let filmgoers everywhere hear an Addis Ababa
      night for the first time. After that came Either/Orchestra and the Heliocentrics, festival
      stages, tours starting again — including the one in the photograph: 2024, eighty-one years old,
      still on stage.
    </p>

    <h2>Straight to the radio</h2>
  </div>

  <p class="mag__cta">
    <a class="mag__more" href={ethiopiaHref}>
      Hear every Ethiopian station
      <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
        <path d="M5 12h13M12 5l7 7-7 7" />
      </svg>
    </a>
  </p>

  <div class="mag__body">
    <h2>Further: follow the thread</h2>
  </div>

  <p class="mag__cta">
    <a class="mag__more" href={wikiUrl} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
      Wikipedia · Mulatu Astatke (the fullest chronology and session list)
      <svg width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.7" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true">
        <path d="M5 12h13M12 5l7 7-7 7" />
      </svg>
    </a>
  </p>

  <p class="mag__outro">
    Now open the Ethiopia page and pick any station. Odds are you will hear contemporary pop with no
    connection to the sixties at all, and that is fine. But once in a while, on some late-night
    programme, you will run into a melody hanging in mid-air: five notes, circling, refusing to land.
    That is the fingerprint this highland country left on the world's music.
  </p>

  <div class="mag__endmatter">
    <div class="mag__endmatter-inner">
      <a class="mag__back" href="/">← Back to home</a>
      <span class="mag__mark">Continental Tour · Voice of the Month · Vol. 02</span>
    </div>
  </div>
</Magazine>
